  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe best way to explain water vapor and radar imagery this morning \nis that there is a wall of water quickly moving our way across the \nPlains. The surface low at 2am was analyzed over northeast Colorado \nand by this evening is forecast to be up around Grand Rapids in \nnorthern MN. This continues the trend of the last couple of days of \nthis initial phase of the storm nudging to the northwest some. Just \nnorth of the low track will be where the heaviest rain falls and the \nmodel consensus on where the heaviest rain falls today (1.5 to 2.5 \ninches) is from the ND/SD/MN border, northeast into the Arrowhead. \nThe MPX area will see a mid level dry slot move in late this \nafternoon, with a lull in precipitation expected tonight as we wait \non phase 2 of this system.\n\nPhase 2 is the slow, lumbering low phase of this system. As the long \nwave trough to our west moves over MN, it will close off into an h5 \nlow that will spin across MN/WI Tuesday and Wednesday. As this \nhappens, the surface low will stall out, with a new low developing \nalong its cold front over southern MN. Model spread for phase 1 \nisn't too bad, but things get dispersive for where this second low \ndevelops and then with how long it takes for it to exit stage right. \nThough differences in the placement of the low exist, where there is \nagreement in the models is that a broad and persistent deformation \nband will wrap around the northwest side of the low. This \ndeformation band will likely result in an additional 1-2\" of rain on \ntop of what we see on Monday. Right now, the favored axis for the \nhighest rain amounts Tuesday and Wednesday is from roughly Redwood \nFalls to Duluth, though this may move around a bit in the next 24 \nhours. What is impressive are the potential rainfall amounts this \nweek where the heaviest rain axes from these two phases of the \nsystem happen to line up. Right now, this looks to be up near the \nheadwaters of the MS River, where amounts of 3-5 inches could be \nseen. This would be at and even exceeding 3 times the normal liquid \nprecipitation seen for the entire month of November in just 3 days. \nAll in all, this looks to be an incredibly beneficial rainfall. \nWith vegetation now mostly dormant and the ground not frozen, this \nrainfall will go to recharging soil moisture and helping stream \nflows and lake levels recover. If the higher end amounts we're \nseeing do hit the headwaters region, this may be the event that can \nfinally break the cycle of droughts that part of the state has been \nstuck in since 2021.\n\nFor Thursday and Friday, we will finally see precipitation push off \nto the east, with high pressure drying us out on Friday. As we have \nseen all fall, the \"cold\" airmass coming in with the high on Friday \nwill mean we see highs back down around normal.\n\nFor the weekend, the GFS has joined the ECMWF in depicting a strong \nshortwave dropping southeast out of Canada, with a clipperesque \nsystem brining another chance of some light rain. Sadly, with its \nretirement planned for November 3rd, we won't get to see how the NAM \nwill handle this system!\n\nBeyond next weekend it's back to ridging and unseasonable warmth, \nwith the storm track shifting to our south as this El Nino continues \nto flex its muscles. The 8-14 outlook from the CPC shows above \nnormal temperatures being likely with any hints of winter in the \nmodels continuing to remain nonexistent.",
